// @error is declared but has no implementation yet
// THE LAST TWO DECLARED-BUT-UNLOWERED MEMORY INTRINSICS. The checker declares
// seventeen; the emitter now lowers fifteen — `__store_i32__`, `__load_i32__`,
// `__log__`, the seven load widths (`__load_i8__`/`__load_u8__`/`__load_i16__`/
// `__load_u16__`/`__load_i64__`/`__load_f32__`/`__load_f64__`), the three WIDE
// store widths (`__store_i64__`/`__store_f32__`/`__store_f64__`),
// `__memory_size__` and `__memory_grow__`.
//
// The two with no emitter arm are `__store_string__` (this one) and
// `__log_string__`. Unlike the three store widths — which were a table entry away
// and are now shipped (`tests/cases/memory/store-widths-round-trip.vl`) — neither
// of these is: `__store_string__` would have to copy a GC `(array i32)` into
// linear memory with no byte encoding decided, and `__log_string__` is a host
// import the native runner does not provide. Each needs a design, not a table row.
//
// THE DIAGNOSTIC IS WHAT THIS FILE PINS. It used to pin
// `@emit-error call to unknown function` — a message that reads like a typo'd
// IDENTIFIER when the identifier is real and the IMPLEMENTATION is what is
// missing, which `docs/internals/memory-gc-design.md` §1.2 filed as "the half
// worth fixing first". The checker now says so itself, positioned at the call,
// on the same unsupported-lowering channel `print` of a reference uses
// (`nameIsUnimplementedIntrinsic`).
//
// SHADOWING IS UNAFFECTED for these two, and deliberately so: they are not
// emitter-intercepted, so a program that DECLARES `function __store_string__(…)`
// still shadows the builtin and still runs — the diagnostic is gated on
// `fnDeclIx` not holding the name. That is NOT true of the three store widths any
// more; acquiring an arm moved them onto the reservation list, where a user
// definition is rejected because it would never run
// (`tests/cases/memory/store-width-user-definition-reserved.vl`).
__store_string__(0, "hi")
print(__load_i32__(0))
